d now we'll fit  a support vector machine but here the  kernel will be radial and we'll give a  cos parameter of five and again we won't  scale the data  okay  and so that's fit  um and so now we're going to create a  grid as before and make predictions on  the grid  now these data actually came supplied  with with grid points if you look down  on the summary  the of the on the names of that one the  the list  there's two variables px1 and px2 and so  these are  a  grid of values for each of the two  variables okay so we don't actually have  to use our function that we created we  can just use expand.grid directly to  create the grid of values and so we do  that  and like before we'll predict  the the classification at each of the  values on the grid  and now we'll plot and and include the  points and color the points according to  the decision boundary  and wow look at that  so you can see the  decision boundary is non-linear now  and  and it's  the the red points are the red regions  largely to the top and the black regions  largely down below we can put  our data points in the plot and we see  where the data lies  and you can see that the the decision  boundaries to to a large extent  following where the data is but in a in  a very non-linear way  okay let's see we if we can improve this  plot a little bit further we'd actually  like to  put in a curve that gives a decision  boundary  and what we're going to do to do that is  we're going to use the contour function  okay  um  on the data on the on the  data frame is also a  variable called prob  and prob actually gives these data was  simulated and prob gives the true  probability of a plus one versus a minus  one at every value on the on the grid  that we have here  okay so we can do two things we're going  to use the contour function and we're  gonna we're gonna plot the contour of  prob at a half which would be the true  decision boundary for these data  and then what we're going to do is we're  going to  we're going to predict from our model  we're going to predict not just the  class label but we're going to predict  the function itself and we'll  we'll get the decision boundary that's  learned for these data by plotting the  contour of that function the zero quant  contour because that function we  classify to plus one if it's positive  otherwise to minus one and so we're  going to do that for each of these and  include them on the plot  okay  so  the first one is we'll just predict our  fit  on the grid  okay but we tell it decision values  equals 2 because we want to get that  function the actual function and not  just the classification  and the way it returns that it returns  it as an attribute of the actual class  of classified values so we have to pull  off that that attribute  and so we use the attributes function  that pulls off all the attributes and  then we we access the one called  decision and so now we have the the  function that we want  and  we  once again we have our grid actually  we've made it already wasn't need to  make it again  and  we um  we've got our classification again so  now we we make the same plot as we had  before  we add our points again and now we'll  make a contour  and we use a contour function and it it  requires those two grid sequences as  well  and then it wants the function you want  to contour in the form of a matrix  and if you look at the dimensions of of  px1 and px2 you'll see they're 69 and 99  and this function we're going to contour  at level 0 and we're going to add it to  the plot  and sure enough you can see that tracks  the decision boundary so that's a really  handy way of plotting a non-linear  decision boundary in in two dimensions  and finally we include the truth  which is the contour of the  probabilities which we're gonna that's  the  0.5 contour  which would be the decision boundary in  terms of the the probabilities and so  that's the true decision boundary or the  what's called the bayes decision  boundary  so that's the ultimate one if we had  large amounts of data we'd hope to be  able to get very close to that but you  can see our non-linear support vector  machine has got pretty close to that  especially in the regions where we've  got data  and and so here we've seen how to  include these non-linear boundaries in  the plots  okay so we've finished the session so  once again we were using r markdown and  we've where we've mixed  actual text with our r commands  the whole the two sessions on svm i like  that and at the end of it all we can use  the function knit  and  it runs through all the code and  produces a nice web document that  summarizes  all that we've done this is something  you can share with your colleagues  um you can display it you can put it on  your own website  puts all the figures in  and you know and and shows exactly what  you've done and all the plots everything  that we produced in somewhat  nice quality as well and so this this is  a document that you can save and and  distribute

